# QuantForge — external VPS deployment (Caddy reverse proxy through an SSH tunnel)
#
# Mirrors the DeepSeek Harness (dsh) remote-gateway pattern already in use on
# this box (see ~/dsh-custom/dsh-web.sh and the kiwi VPS Caddyfile):
#
#   phone / outside ──HTTPS─► Caddy (kiwi VPS :8445, basic-auth login)
#                                  │ reverse_proxy 127.0.0.1:9445
#                                  ▼
#                          SSH reverse tunnel (-R 9445:8000)
#                                  │
#                                  ▼
#                     local QuantForge FastAPI backend (:8000, HTTPS self-signed)
#                     ─ serves both /api and the built React SPA (frontend/dist)
#
# Caddy on the VPS terminates TLS (Let's Encrypt cert for
# 74-211-104-188.nip.io) and is the real authentication layer: a password
# login guards the whole site. Only the read-only optimize-progress WebSocket
# (/api/ws/*) is exempt, because browsers do not attach cached HTTP-auth
# credentials to WebSocket handshakes — same reason dsh exempts /api/events.*.

# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# PUBLIC URL
#   https://74-211-104-188.nip.io:8445
#   login:  user dsh, password = <QUANTFORGE_LOGIN_PASSWORD> (see deploy.sh)
# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# SITE BLOCK — append this to the kiwi VPS /etc/caddy/Caddyfile
# (the basic_auth entry uses a PLACEHOLDER for the bcrypt hash. Generate the
#  real hash ON THE VPS with `caddy hash-password --plaintext '<password>'`
#  and substitute it in place of the placeholder. Never commit a real hash.)
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# quantforge remote gateway.
# Caddy terminates TLS on :8445, then proxies to the SSH reverse tunnel
# listener at 127.0.0.1:9445 (which carries traffic to the laptop's QuantForge
# dashboard backend on :8000). The backend is HTTPS with a self-signed
# localhost cert, so the reverse proxy skips TLS verification (Caddy is the
# public TLS terminator).
#
# /api/* (data + live-trading controls, and the /api/ws/* optimize progress
# WebSocket) is exempt from the Caddy basic_auth: browsers do NOT attach cached
# HTTP-auth credentials to fetch()/WebSocket requests, so protecting /api would
# 401 every API call and break the SPA (areason dsh exempts its /api/events.*
# streams, and comfyui exempts /api/* the same way). The page + static assets
# still sit behind the password login — the password gates *entering the app*.
# https://74-211-104-188.nip.io:8445 {
# 	@needs_auth not path /api/*
# 	basic_auth @needs_auth {
# 		dsh $2a$14$<bcrypt-hash-of-QUANTFORGE_LOGIN_PASSWORD>   # TODO(deploy): substitute via `caddy hash-password`
# 	}
# 	reverse_proxy 127.0.0.1:9445 {
# 		transport http {
# 			tls_insecure_skip_verify
# 		}
# 	}
# }

# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# NOTES
# - The backend must already be running locally (prod mode serves the SPA from
#   apps/dashboard/frontend/dist). See apps/dashboard/start.sh --prod.
# - If the backend later binds 127.0.0.1 only, the tunnel line stays identical
#   (`-R 127.0.0.1:9445:127.0.0.1:8000`); only drop tls_insecure_skip_verify if
#   the backend serves plain HTTP, and reverse_proxy to http://127.0.0.1:9445.
# - Caddy's automatic HTTPS obtains/reuses a Let's Encrypt cert for
#   74-211-104-188.nip.io, so the public URL needs no self-signed CA on phones.
